# XGSPON Feature Development Workflow

When the user provides a **feature description** (JIRA ticket, spec, or verbal requirement), follow this end-to-end workflow. Do not skip phases; output a structured task breakdown before coding.

---

## 1. Task Breakdown (from feature description)

Parse the feature and produce a checklist covering four phases:

| Phase | Deliverable |
|-------|-------------|
| **Framework design** | Affected repos/modules, data model (YANG/proto), API boundaries, call flow, dependencies, risk |
| **Coding** | Concrete file/function changes per repo, branch name, **DbgCli commands** (if applicable), commit plan |
| **Review** | Self-review checklist, CR text draft (see `xgs-cr-review.mdc`), commit message draft (see `xgs-git-commit.mdc`) |
| **Compile & verify** | Recipe(s) to rebuild, bitbake commands, on-device or unit verification steps |

Identify which repos are touched before writing code. A single feature often spans multiple trees (e.g. YANG change → netconf-polt handler → xpon-apps daemon).

---

## 2. Framework Design

Before implementation, document:

1. **Scope** — operator-visible behavior, TR-383/TR-385/WT-451 touch points if NETCONF-related
2. **Data model** — new/changed YANG nodes (`xpon-yang`), protobuf messages (`xpon-protos`), sysrepo datastore paths
3. **Layer map** — which layer owns the logic:
   - YANG/sysrepo config → `netconf-polt` NETCONF modules
   - OLT hardware/BAL → read `sw-bcm686olt` (read-only); implement in `netconf-polt/balapi`
   - Background daemons → `xpon-apps`
   - Shared utilities → `xpon-libs`
4. **Interface contracts** — gRPC (vOMCI), IPC, Redis, BAL API calls between components
5. **Debug surface** — plan DbgCli commands (`dump`/`get`/`enable`/`status`) if feature adds runtime state worth field inspection
6. **Build order** — e.g. `xpon-yang` → `xpon-protos` → `xpon-libs` → `netconf-polt` → `xpon-apps`

Keep design proportional to feature size; skip heavy design docs for trivial one-file fixes.

---

## 3. Coding

### Paths

| Item | Path |
|------|------|
| Project root (Yocto) | `~/works/repo` (`/home/oreo/works/repo`) |
| Application source (externalsrc) | `~/works/repo/build-xpon/workspace/sources/` |
| bbappend (devtool overrides) | `~/works/repo/build-xpon/workspace/appends/` |
| Build dir (after setup-env) | `~/works/repo/build-xpon/` |

### Git branch policy (mandatory)

- **Forbidden**: develop or `git push` directly on **`hank-dev`** in any source repo.
- **Required**: work on a personal branch whose name **starts with `Oreo/`** (e.g. `Oreo/XGS-9899-PerPon-Indication`).
- Before editing, run `git branch --show-current` in each affected repo; create/checkout `Oreo/<JIRA>-<short-desc>` from updated `hank-dev` if needed.
- Push only to `origin Oreo/...`; merge to `hank-dev` via PR/MR per team process.

Each source tree under `workspace/sources/` is an **independent git repo** — check branch status per repo.

### Read-only repos (mandatory — no edits)

The following trees under `~/works/repo/build-xpon/workspace/sources/` are **read-only**:

| Directory | Path | Allowed | Forbidden |
|-----------|------|---------|-----------|
| `sw-bcm686olt` | `sources/sw-bcm686olt/` | Read, search, trace APIs, learn BAL/host_driver behavior | Any file edit, patch, commit, or push |
| `libyang` | `sources/libyang/` | Read upstream YANG parser/validator implementation | Any file edit, patch, commit, or push |
| `sysrepo` | `sources/sysrepo/` | Read datastore/plugin implementation for understanding | Any file edit, patch, commit, or push |

- Use these repos **only for code learning and understanding** how lower layers work.
- If a feature appears to require changes in these trees, **stop and propose alternatives** in editable repos (e.g. `netconf-polt/balapi` for BAL integration, `netconf-polt` for sysrepo usage, `xpon-yang` for model changes).
- Do **not** include these repos in coding plans, bitbake `-c compile -f` iteration, or CR diffs unless the user explicitly overrides this rule.

### Code style — learn before edit (mandatory)

Before writing or modifying any file:

1. **Read surrounding code** in the same file and sibling files in that module — match indentation, brace style, naming, and comment style exactly.
2. **Follow project standards**: RnD0045 (`xgs-coding.mdc`), logging (`xgs-logging.mdc`).
3. **Match module conventions** — observe per-repo patterns:
   - `netconf-polt`: Vecima copyright header, `bcmos_*` / `BCM_*` types, `BCM_LOG()` / module log IDs, static helpers in module `.c` files
   - `xpon-apps` / `xpon-libs`: `LOG()` macro via `xponutils/logger.h`, `RET_OK` / `RET_ERROR`, snake_case handlers
4. **Minimal diff** — no drive-by formatting, rename, or refactor outside the feature scope.
5. **Whitespace** — truly empty blank lines; no trailing spaces; one newline at EOF.

Use read-only repos (`sw-bcm686olt`, `libyang`, `sysrepo`) as **style/API reference** when tracing calls into lower layers.

### DbgCli / DBC debug interfaces (add when appropriate)

Runtime debug is exposed via **DbgCli** (DBC) — the remote/local debug console attached to each daemon. API: `xpon-libs/debug/include/dbg.h`.

**When to add DBC commands** for a new feature:

- New runtime state, counters, caches, or flags worth inspecting on a live OLT
- `dump` / `get` of internal structures (complement to NETCONF `xpon get …`)
- `enable` / `disable` / `status` toggles for debug logging or simulation paths
- Test hooks (alarm simulation, forced state transitions) used in field debug

Skip DBC for pure config/YANG-only changes with no new runtime state.

**Where to implement**:

| Component | DbgCli file | Command table |
|-----------|-------------|---------------|
| NETCONF / bbf-xpon | `netconf-polt/netconf_server/modules/bbf-xpon/bbf-debug.c` | `gCmsDbgCliCommands[]`, `gCmsDbgCliHelpText` |
| protocol-handler | `xpon-apps/protocol-handler/common/src/ph_dbg.c` | same pattern |
| metrics-mgr, node-mgr, … | `<app>/src/*_dbg.c` or `main.c` | per-app `*DbgCliCmds[]` |

**Handler pattern** (follow existing handlers in `bbf-debug.c` / `ph_dbg.c`):

```c
static int dbg_my_feature_handler(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    if (argc < 1 || argv == NULL || argv[0] == NULL)
    {
        PRINT_USAGE_EXIT("Usage: my_feature <get|dump|enable|disable|status> ...\n");
    }
    /* parse argv[0] action; validate argc for each branch */
    return RET_OK;   /* or RET_ERROR */
}
```

**Registration checklist**:

1. Add `{"my_feature", dbg_my_feature_handler}` to the app's `DbgCliCmdListEntryT` array (before `{NULL, NULL}`).
2. Append usage line(s) to the matching `*HelpText` string.
3. Keep `DBG_CLI_CMD_LIST_BASIC_ENTRIES` and `DBG_CLI_CMD_LIST_LOG_ENTRIES` in the table.
4. Prefer extending existing commands (e.g. new `xpon get <type>`) when the feature fits an existing namespace.
5. **Never SIGSEGV on empty/partial input** — always validate `argc` / `argv` and print Usage (see `xpon` no-arg crash fix).

**Verify**: connect DbgCli to the daemon (`DBG_CLI_APP_NETCONF` for netconf-polt), run new command with no args → Usage printed, process stays alive.

### Coding standards (summary)

- Match existing style (see above); minimal, task-scoped diffs.
- Logging per `xgs-logging.mdc`.
- Include DBC commands in CR test plan when added.

---

## 4. Review

Before declaring done:

1. **Self-review** — logic, edge cases, memory/lock safety (C/C++), YANG constraint alignment
2. **Code style** — diff matches surrounding module conventions; no unrelated formatting
3. **DbgCli** — new commands registered, help text updated, empty-input returns Usage (no crash)
4. **CR text** — draft using `xgs-cr-review.mdc` template (JIRA link, summary, changes, test plan incl. DBC steps)
5. **Commit message** — `XGS-##### <imperative subject>` + bullet body per `xgs-git-commit.mdc`
6. **Cross-repo consistency** — YANG ↔ handler ↔ proto ↔ app agree on field names and semantics

Do not commit unless the user explicitly asks.

---

## 5. Compile & Verify

### Environment setup (every new shell)

```bash
cd ~/works/repo
. setup-env          # note: leading dot; lands in build-xpon/
```

`MACHINE` is typically `exs1610`, `DISTRO` is `xpon` (see `build-xpon/conf/local.conf`).

### Module compile (iterative development)

After editing externalsrc, force recompile to avoid stale sstate:

```bash
bitbake <recipe> -c compile -f
bitbake <recipe>
```

| Recipe | Source tree | Role / key output |
|--------|-------------|---------------------|
| `netconf-polt` | `sources/netconf-polt` | NETCONF server `bcmolt_netconf_server`; TR-383/385/451 YANG handlers; ONU mgmt; vOMCI gRPC |
| `xpon-apps` | `sources/xpon-apps` | OLT daemons: protocol-handler, node-mgr, event-monitor, metrics-mgr, logsvr, swmgr, callhome, notif-mgr, … |
| `xpon-libs` | `sources/xpon-libs` | Shared libs: ipc, redis, log, timer, taskman, utils, dpl, leds |
| `xpon-protos` | `sources/xpon-protos` | gRPC/protobuf definitions and generated C/C++ stubs (`proto/`) |
| `xpon-yang` | `sources/xpon-yang` | YANG models (bbf TR-383/385, ietf, exs1610); consumed by NETCONF/sysrepo |
| `sw-bcm686olt` | `sources/sw-bcm686olt/release` | **Read-only.** Broadcom BAL SDK / host_driver reference (IDE root: `release/`) |
| `libyang` | `sources/libyang` | **Read-only.** Upstream YANG parser/validator (CESNET) |
| `sysrepo` | `sources/sysrepo` | **Read-only.** Upstream YANG configuration datastore |

bbappend files in `workspace/appends/` map each recipe to its externalsrc path via `EXTERNALSRC:pn-<recipe>`.

### Full image / release bundle

```bash
bitbake xpon-olt-bundle
```

Use for release validation or RAUC bundle; prefer module compile during feature iteration.

### netconf-polt quick verify

See `netconf-polt-rebuild-verify.mdc`: deploy `bcmolt_netconf_server` to OLT, restart `netconf-polt.service`, smoke-test DbgCli.

### IDE navigation (optional)

From `workspace/sources/`: `./setup-compile-commands.sh` after bitbake builds; open `xpon-externalsrc.code-workspace` for clangd.

